sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-44> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "If the theorems of basic logic are listed in order of increasing complexity, the ones that cannot be derived from simpler theorems earlier in the list are almost exactly the ones traditional textbooks single out and name as interesting.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Named textbook theorems of logic are those not derivable from simpler ones";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “Implications for Mathematics and Its Foundations” (from p. 772), p. 818. Scope: theorems of propositional logic and the named laws in logic textbooks"
}
